From c8d3571bea79f8c66bccd702469ea219b3226086 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: patch <patch@local>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:22:12 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] docs: describe experimental SuperGrok sign-in storage

---
 docs/PRIVACY.md | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/docs/PRIVACY.md b/docs/PRIVACY.md
index 0c3d2cd..85c83f1 100644
--- a/docs/PRIVACY.md
+++ b/docs/PRIVACY.md
@@ -51,6 +51,7 @@ Smart Vault's index (recognised text, page text, meaning vectors and job state)
 - ChatGPT sign-in (from v0.8.3.0-beta.1) uses OAuth with PKCE in the system browser. For the length of one sign-in only, Orbit listens on `127.0.0.1` (port 1455, or 1457 if busy) for OpenAI's redirect, checks that it carries the exact random state Orbit created, exchanges the code, and closes the listener. Nothing off the phone can reach that address. The PKCE verifier is kept only in memory. The one-time-code sign-in remains available as a fallback, and Orbit never switches between the two on its own.
 - The provider, model and strength a request is sent with are chosen by the user per chat. Orbit does not route a request to a different model, and when an account cannot use a model Orbit says so rather than answering with another one. The one exception is a chat the user sets to Auto, where Orbit chooses among the providers the user allowed, before sending, and records the choice.
 - Anthropic and xAI developer API keys are encrypted with provider-specific Android Keystore keys, with no plaintext fallback. They are never redisplayed after saving, placed in model metadata, sent to another provider, logged, diagnosed or backed up.
+- (Fork patch, experimental) xAI can alternatively be connected with SuperGrok device-code sign-in at `auth.x.ai`. The resulting access and refresh tokens are encrypted with their own Android Keystore key, with no plaintext fallback, excluded from backups, never logged or shown. Sign-out deletes them from the phone (server-side revocation is not implemented). The client id, endpoints and scope are unofficial and unverified, and API access may be refused (HTTP 403) on some subscription tiers.
 - OpenRouter (from v0.8.3.0-beta.6) is connected by signing in on OpenRouter's own page in the system browser (OAuth with S256 PKCE) or, as a fallback, by entering an API key. During one sign-in only, Orbit listens on a random port on the phone's loopback address (`127.0.0.1`, and `::1` where available) for OpenRouter's redirect, accepts only the exact random state Orbit put in that address, exchanges the one-time code once, and closes the listener. The PKCE verifier and state stay in memory. The resulting user-controlled key, like a typed one, is Keystore-encrypted with no plaintext fallback, never redisplayed, logged, diagnosed or backed up. Disconnecting removes it from the phone; revoking the key itself is done in the OpenRouter account.
 - Extension `secret` and `secret_url` fields are encrypted with Android Keystore-backed AES/GCM and fail closed if secure storage is unavailable.
 - The optional private-relay access token prefers Keystore-backed encryption. Current compatibility behavior can fall back to Orbit's app-private preferences if Keystore persistence fails. Do not reuse a high-value provider key as this relay token; keep the provider key on the relay server.
